//? Title: Spigot e: actual factorial carries and a certified prefix
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Decimal digits of e must be emitted in order without substituting a
floating-point constant. A factorial-base state can be multiplied by ten and
renormalized so each outgoing carry is the next digit. We expose actual states,
retain the original40-digit prefix and400-fractional-digit histogram, and
certify the emitted prefix with exact rational bounds.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Start from e=2+sum(k=2..infinity)1/k!. With m retained factorial-base columns
initialized to1, multiply the state by10 and propagate carry from column m down
to2, storing remainder modulo each column base. The carry leaving column2 is the
next actual emitted decimal digit. The working state suffices to produce later
digits; already emitted output is not used to compute new ones.

A finite mixed-radix truncation needs a guard argument. Instead of assuming
m=d+20 suffices, the native producer independently bounds the tail:

$$ S_m=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac1{k!},\qquad S_m<e<S_m+\frac1{m\,m!}. $$
\alttext(The finite factorial series is a strict lower bound for e, and adding one over m times m factorial is a strict upper bound.)

For m>=1, compare successive tail denominators with a geometric series to obtain
the upper bound. Scale both rational endpoints by10^d and require their integer
floors to agree with the actual emitted prefix. This certifies truncation;
unresolved intervals fail explicitly. The mixed-radix loop uses bounded small
integers, but the added certificate uses arbitrary-precision integers, so the
entire routine is not a small-integer-only algorithm.

There are d*(d+19) actual column visits for m=d+20. Storage is linear without
optional complete state histories; recording every state is quadratic.
Arbitrary-precision certification adds separate bit work not included in the
column counter. A short digit histogram cannot establish normality or randomness
of e.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spigot_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::spigot_e_trace(5);
    std::print("| actual digit index | carry digit | actual factorial-state columns2..m |\n| ---: | ---: | " "--- |\n");
    for (const auto &s : r.history) {
        const std::vector<int> state(s.remainders.begin() + 2,
                                     s.remainders.end());
        std::print("| {} | {} | `{}` |\n", s.index, s.digit, state);
    }
    std::print("\nActual emitted `{}`, certified {}.\n", r.digits, r.certified);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Factorial state initialized to ones");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual column updates",
                                     r.column_updates));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Certified prefix {}", r.digits));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/spigot_tiny.svg");
}
```
